- vercel:next-forge: Expert assistance for next-forge — a production-grade Turborepo  (file: r4/next-forge/upstream/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:next-upgrade: Upgrade Next.js to the latest version following official migrat (file: r4/next-upgrade/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:next-upgrade: Upgrade Next.js to the latest version following official migra (file: r4/next-upgrade/upstream/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:nextjs: Next.js App Router expert guidance. Use when building, debuggin (file: r4/nextjs/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:react-best-practices: React best-practices reviewer for TSX files. Triggers after edi (file: r4/react-best-practices/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:routing-middleware: Vercel Routing Middleware guidance — request interception before  (file: r4/routing-middleware/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:runtime-cache: Vercel Runtime Cache API guidance — ephemeral per-region key-va (file: r4/runtime-cache/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:shadcn: shadcn/ui expert guidance — CLI, component installation, composit (file: r4/shadcn/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:turbopack: Turbopack expert guidance. Use when configuring the Next.js bundl (file: r4/turbopack/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:vercel-agent: Vercel Agent guidance — AI-powered code review, incident investig (file: r4/vercel-agent/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:vercel-cli: Vercel CLI expert guidance. Use when deploying, managing enviro (file: r4/vercel-cli/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:vercel-cli: Deploy, manage, and develop projects on Vercel from the comman (file: r4/vercel-cli/upstream/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:vercel-firewall: Vercel Firewall expert guidance — automatic DDoS mitigation, th (file: r4/vercel-firewall/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:vercel-functions: Vercel Functions expert guidance — Serverless Functions, Edge Fun (file: r4/vercel-functions/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:vercel-react-best-practices: React and Next.js performance optimization guidelines from Verc (file: r4/react-best-practices/upstream/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:vercel-sandbox: Vercel Sandbox guidance — ephemeral Firecracker microVMs for runn (file: r4/vercel-sandbox/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:vercel-sandbox: Run agent-browser + Chrome inside Vercel Sandbox microVMs for  (file: r4/vercel-sandbox/upstream/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:vercel-storage: Vercel storage expert guidance — Blob, Edge Config, and Marketpla (file: r4/vercel-storage/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:verification: Full-story verification — infers what the user is building, then  (file: r4/verification/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:workflow: Vercel Workflow DevKit (WDK) expert guidance. Use when building (file: r4/workflow/SKILL.md)\n- vercel:workflow: Creates durable, resumable workflows using Vercel's Workflow D (file: r4/workflow/upstream/SKILL.md)\n### How to use skills\n- Discovery: The list above is the skills available in this session (name + description + short path). Skill bodies live on disk at the listed paths after expanding the matching alias from `### Skill roots`.\n- Trigger rules: If the user names a skill (with `$SkillName` or plain text) OR the task clearly matches a skill's description shown above, you must use that skill for that turn. Multiple mentions mean use them all. Do not carry skills across turns unless re-mentioned.\n- Missing/blocked: If a named skill isn't in the list or the path can't be read, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n- How to use a skill (progressive disclosure):\n  1) After deciding to use a skill, expand the listed short `path` with the matching alias from `### Skill roots`, then open its `SKILL.md`. Read only enough to follow the workflow.\n  2) When `SKILL.md` references relative paths (e.g., `scripts/foo.py`), resolve them relative to the directory containing that expanded `SKILL.md` first, and only consider other paths if needed.\n  3) If `SKILL.md` points to extra folders such as `references/`, load only the specific files needed for the request; don't bulk-load everything.\n  4) If `scripts/` exist, prefer running or patching them instead of retyping large code blocks.\n  5) If `assets/` or templates exist, reuse them instead of recreating from scratch.\n- Coordination and sequencing:\n  - If multiple skills apply, choose the minimal set that covers the request and state the order you'll use them.\n  - Announce which skill(s) you're using and why (one short line). If you skip an obvious skill, say why.\n- Context hygiene:\n  - Keep context small: summarize long sections instead of pasting them; only load extra files when needed.\n  - Avoid deep reference-chasing: prefer opening only files directly linked from `SKILL.md` unless you're blocked.\n  - When variants exist (frameworks, providers, domains), pick only the relevant reference file(s) and note that choice.\n- Safety and fallback: If a skill can't be applied cleanly (missing files, unclear instructions), state the issue, pick the next-best approach, and continue.\n</skills_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<plugins_instructions>\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps. Below is the list of plugins that are enabled and available in this session.\n### Available plugins\n- `Browser`: Browser / browser-use plugin Aliases: @browser, @browser-use, browser-use, Browser, in-app browser. Use Browser, the Codex in-app browser, when the user asks to open, inspect, navigate, test, click, type, or screenshot local web targets such as localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, file:// URLs, or the current in-app browser tab. After significant frontend changes to a local app, use Browser to open the relevant local target when it is known or obvious, unless the user asks for another browser tool. For requests like \"open localhost:3000\" or \"open to localhost:4000\", navigate the in-app browser to http://localhost:3000 or http://localhost:4000. Do not satisfy explicit @browser or @browser-use requests with macOS `open`, shell commands, or generic web browsing unless the user asks for another browser tool or approves a fallback.\n- `Chrome`: Chrome automation for remote URLs, authenticated/profile-dependent pages, existing Chrome tabs, cookies, extensions, and Codex Chrome Extension setup.\n- `Documents`: Create and edit document artifacts in Codex, including Word files and Google Docs.\n- `Google Drive`: Use Google Drive as the single entrypoint for Drive, Docs, Sheets, and Slides work.\n- `Presentations`: Create, edit, render, verify, and export presentation slide decks. Use when Codex needs to build or modify a deck, slidedeck, presentation deck, slide deck, slides, PowerPoint, Google Slides, PPT, PPTX, .ppt, or .pptx file.\n- `Slack`: Work with Slack using the configured Slack integration.\n- `Spreadsheets`: Create, edit, analyze, visualize, render, and export spreadsheets or Google Sheets-ready workbooks in Codex.\n- `telegram`: Telegram channel for Claude Code — messaging bridge with built-in access control. Manage pairing, allowlists, and policy via /telegram:access.\n- `vercel`: Build and deploy web apps and agents\n### How to use plugins\n- Discovery: The list above is the plugins available in this session.\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Preference: When a relevant plugin is available, prefer using capabilities associated with that plugin over standalone capabilities that provide similar functionality.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that is not listed above, or the plugin does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n</plugins_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"## Memory\n\nYou have access to a memory folder with guidance from prior runs. It can save\ntime and help you stay consistent. Use it whenever it is likely to help.\n\nDecision boundary: should you use memory for a new user query?\n\n- Skip memory ONLY when the request is clearly self-contained and does not need\n  workspace history, conventions, or prior decisions.\n- Hard skip examples: current time/date, simple translation, simple sentence\n  rewrite, one-line shell command, trivial formatting.\n- Use memory by default when ANY of these are true:\n  - the query mentions workspace/repo/module/path/files in MEMORY_SUMMARY below,\n  - the user asks for prior context / consistency / previous decisions,\n  - the task is ambiguous and could depend on earlier project choices,\n  - the ask is a non-trivial and related to MEMORY_SUMMARY below.\n- If unsure, do a quick memory pass.\n\nMemory layout (general -> specific):\n\n- $HOME/.codex/memories/memory_summary.md (already provided below; do NOT open again)\n- $HOME/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md (searchable registry; primary file to query)\n- $HOME/.codex/memories/skills/<skill-name>/ (skill folder)\n  - SKILL.md (entrypoint instructions)\n  - scripts/ (optional helper scripts)\n  - examples/ (optional example outputs)\n  - templates/ (optional templates)\n- $HOME/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ (per-rollout recaps + evidence snippets)\n  - The paths of these entries can be found in $HOME/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md or $HOME/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ as `rollout_path`\n  - These files are append-only `jsonl`: `session_meta.payload.id` identifies the session, `turn_context` marks turn boundaries, `event_msg` is the lightweight status stream, and `response_item` contains actual messages, tool calls, and tool outputs.\n  - For efficient lookup, prefer matching the filename suffix or `session_meta.payload.id`; avoid broad full-content scans unless needed.\n\nQuick memory pass (when applicable):\n\n1. Skim the MEMORY_SUMMARY below and extract task-relevant keywords.\n2. Search $HOME/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md using those keywords.\n3. Only if MEMORY.md directly points to rollout summaries/skills, open the 1-2\n   most relevant files under $HOME/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/ or\n   $HOME/.codex/memories/skills/.\n4. If above are not clear and you need exact commands, error text, or precise evidence, search over `rollout_path` for more evidence.\n5. If there are no relevant hits, stop memory lookup and continue normally.\n\nQuick-pass budget:\n\n- Keep memory lookup lightweight: ideally <= 4-6 search steps before main work.\n- Avoid broad scans of all rollout summaries.\n\nDuring execution: if you hit repeated errors, confusing behavior, or suspect\nrelevant prior context, redo the quick memory pass.\n\nHow to decide whether to verify memory:\n\n- Consider both risk of drift and verification effort.\n- If a fact is likely to drift and is cheap to verify, verify it before\n  answering.\n- If a fact is likely to drift but verification is expensive, slow, or\n  disruptive, it is acceptable to answer from memory in an interactive turn,\n  but you should say that it is memory-derived, note that it may be stale, and\n  consider offering to refresh it live.\n- If a fact is lower-drift and expensive to verify, it is usually fine to\n  answer from memory directly.\n\nWhen answering from memory without current verification:\n\n- If you rely on memory for a fact that you did not verify in the current turn,\n  say so briefly in the final answer.\n- If that fact is plausibly drift-prone or comes from an older note, older\n  snapshot, or prior run summary, say that it may be stale or outdated.\n- If live verification was skipped and a refresh would be useful in the\n  interactive context, consider offering to verify or refresh it live.\n- Do not present unverified memory-derived facts as confirmed-current.\n- Prefer a short refresh offer for interactive questions, especially about prior\n  results, commands, timing, or older snapshots.\n\nMemory citation requirements:\n\n- If ANY relevant memory files were used: append exactly one\n`<oai-mem-citation>` block as the VERY LAST content of the final reply.\n  Normal responses should include the answer first, then append the\n`<oai-mem-citation>` block at the end.\n- Use this exact structure for programmatic parsing:\n```\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-236|note=[responsesapi citation extraction code pointer]\nrollout_summaries/2026-02-17T21-23-02-LN3m-example.md:10-12|note=[weekly report format]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043\n019c7714-3b77-74d1-9866-e1f484aae2ab\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>\n```\n- `citation_entries` is for rendering:\n  - one citation entry per line\n  - format: `<file>:<line_start>-<line_end>|note=[<how memory was used>]`\n  - use file paths relative to the memory base path (for example, `MEMORY.md`,\n    `rollout_summaries/...`, `skills/...`)\n  - only cite files actually used under the memory base path (do not cite\n    workspace files as memory citations)\n  - if you used `MEMORY.md` and then a rollout summary/skill file, cite both\n  - list entries in order of importance (most important first)\n  - `note` should be short, single-line, and use simple characters only (avoid\n    unusual symbols, no newlines)\n- `rollout_ids` is for us to track what previous rollouts you find useful:\n  - include one rollout id per line\n  - rollout ids should look like UUIDs (for example,\n    `019c6e27-e55b-73d1-87d8-4e01f1f75043`)\n  - include unique ids only; do not repeat ids\n  - an empty `<rollout_ids>` section is allowed if no rollout ids are available\n  - you can find rollout ids in rollout summary files and MEMORY.md\n  - do not include file paths or notes in this section\n  - For every `citation_entries`, try to find and cite the corresponding rollout id if possible\n- Never include memory citations inside pull-request messages.\n- Never cite blank lines; double-check ranges.\n\nUpdating memories:\n\nYou can update the memories **only** when explicitly asked by the user. This must always come from a direct request from the user.\n- Write your update in $HOME/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n- Each update must be one small file containing what you want to add/delete/update from the memories.\n- The name of this file must be `<timestamp>-<short slug>.md`\n- Do not try to edit the memory files yourself, only add one update note in $HOME/.codex/memories/extensions/ad_hoc/notes/\n\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY BEGINS =========\nv1\n\n## User Profile\n\nJarrad is driving a parallel four-app v1 push across `Sandra`, `Closer Lab`, `BMH Institute`, and `Jitter`. He uses Codex as an executor for repo worktrees, browser proof, and local automation checks, and he expects real evidence over plausible explanation. He works across sibling repos under `$HOME/Sites/*`, so cwd boundaries, worktree ownership, and proof status matter.\n\nHe repeatedly pushes toward deeper autonomy with tighter control: keep moving through the next safe unblocked step, but do not hide blockers, drift, or partial proof. For browser work he prefers a small number of stable Chrome tabs, not tab sprawl or browser-capable subagents opening fresh surfaces. For recon/planning work he often asks for read-only, concise-but-granular reports with actionable matrices rather than broad prose.\n\nHe is security-conscious around secrets and shared infrastructure. Shared test credentials should use the approved 1Password service-account path, not chat or vault notes. Home-level hooks and cross-session automations are treated as autonomy-affecting and need explicit approval if they expand beyond the approved BMH-OS boundary. [ad-hoc note]\n\n## User preferences\n\n- Continue through the next safe unblocked step; do not stop at leaf-task boundaries. If a stop is real, cite the exact rule and give the concrete path forward. [ad-hoc note]\n- If explicit approval is the blocker, include the exact copy-ready approval prompt with scope, allowed action, evidence requirements, and hard boundaries. [ad-hoc note]\n- Keep browser work in a small set of stable Chrome tabs. The user explicitly objected to \"way too many props\" and called new-tab churn \"anti-autonomous.\"\n- In the three-app Browser V1 lane, do not restart Closer from old fake-mic forensics when the remembered next step is already one approved real-microphone Chrome proof with redacted evidence capture. [ad-hoc note]\n- For browser/debugging/review work, prefer Google Chrome and Chrome DevTools over the Codex in-app browser when signal quality matters. [ad-hoc note]\n- In Jitter browser verification and similar planning, default to exhaustive matrix coverage: fixture models, personas, roles, actions, edge paths, and evidence packets.\n- When he asks for read-only recon, keep it strictly non-mutating and produce a concise but granular report once enough evidence is gathered.\n- Treat durable test data and credential persistence as first-class planning requirements when he says he wants to \"play around with it too.\"\n- Use the approved 1Password service-account flow for shared credentials; do not print secret values, ask for pasted secrets, or rely on GUI auth.\n- For Jitter PRs, automatically do manual code review, fix valid in-scope findings, rerun verification, and generate the Claude handoff. [ad-hoc note]\n- When asked to paste something into Claude Code desktop, prepare it but do not send until timing is explicit. [ad-hoc note]\n\n## General Tips\n\n- `raw_memories.md` now contains the main June 2 rollout evidence. Search it first for thread ids, rollout filenames, and task-local wording before diving into summaries.\n- Treat `extensions/ad_hoc/instructions.md` as authoritative for the ad-hoc notes extension, and tag any summary-layer facts derived from those notes with `[ad-hoc note]`.\n- For Closer Lab Browser V1, separate code-inferred, mock-browser, and real-provider/browser proof. Many recon facts are strong, but they are not the same as production browser verification.\n- For four-app v1 browser work, keep browser execution controller-only unless explicitly re-authorized; clean up temporary credential artifacts after use.\n- Vault raw operational captures belong in `~/BMH-OS/_inbox/`; log Codex writes in `~/BMH-OS/_meta/log.md`.\n- Quick local automation checks should use the real runtime surfaces: `~/.codex/automations/.../automation.toml`, `launchctl print`, `_inbox` mtimes, and `_meta/capture.log`.\n\n## What's in Memory\n\n### $HOME/Sites/BMH apps\n\n#### 2026-06-02\n\n- Four-app v1 browser continuation, Closer real-mic next step, and tab-sprawl correction: Closer Lab, BMH Institute, Jitter, controller-only browser, stable tabs, real-microphone, fake-mic, PR #79, Invalid email or password\n  - desc: Search this first for the active cross-app browser rollout, what was visibly proven versus interrupted, the stable-tabs rule, and the current Closer resume point inside `cwd=$HOME/Sites/BMH apps`.\n  - learnings: The highest-signal deltas are controller-only browser and Closer resume state: fake-mic proved route transport but not learner transcript, so the next step is one approved real-microphone Chrome proof rather than restarting PR `#78`/fake-mic loops. [ad-hoc note]\n\n- Jitter browser-verification planning and 1Password-backed fixtures: exhaustive plan, persona matrix, fixture matrix, /dialer, /cockpit, /audit, /twilio-probe, Claude CLI review\n  - desc: Use for Jitter planning before browser execution when the user wants an exhaustive Chrome-based verification prompt, durable canary data, and secure shared credentials.\n  - learnings: Start with matrices, not prose; durable fixtures plus 1Password persistence are part of the initial plan, and Claude CLI review is useful only after manual reconciliation.\n\n- Sandra CRM backup and vault capture: Sandra CRM, skip trace, copflsklaefwzipsrjqz, PITR, logical export, ~/BMH-OS/_inbox\n  - desc: Covers the Sandra CRM recoverability question, the owner-only logical export, and the exact vault placement/logging pattern for raw backup captures.\n  - learnings: Focus on CRM record recoverability, not generic architecture; physical backups existed, PITR was off, and the logical export lived outside the repo with per-table verification.\n\n- BMH-OS lane watcher and inbox capture health: four-app-v1-lane-watcher, NO MATERIAL CHANGE, automation.toml, com.bmh.os.capture, StartInterval 900\n  - desc: Use for local runtime checks when the question is whether recurring automation should skip work or whether the vault inbox capture loop is healthy.\n  - learnings: Cheap fingerprint gating belongs in the real automation config, and a single `summarize-error` is not a broken capture loop if `_inbox` writes and `capture.log` continue.\n\n### Closer Lab Browser V1 recon worktrees\n\n#### 2026-06-02\n\n- Closer Lab Browser V1 read-only recon and fixture architecture: schema, RLS, RuntimeContainer, claim_score_slot, SHARE_TOKEN_PATTERN, HS256 JWT, destructive reset, BMH Secrets\n  - desc: Search this for the June 2 Closer Lab recon cluster spanning schema, admin/member routes, runtime/provider/scoring behavior, share/embed contracts, fixture modularity, destructive cleanup, and Browser V1 credential hardening.\n  - learnings: The strongest reusable output is the gap map: current seeds are partial, browser states must be modeled separately from mock/real-provider proof, and cleanup/credential handling have stricter boundaries than the existing scripts imply.\n\n### workflow scope on local macOS host and ~/BMH-OS\n\n#### 2026-06-02\n\n- Cross-repo blocker handling and approval prompts: actual blocker, next safe unblocked step, copy-ready approval prompt, scope, evidence requirements, hard boundaries\n  - desc: Search this first when a run reaches a real approval gate and you need the right blocker handoff behavior across `$HOME/Sites/*` workflows. [ad-hoc note]\n  - learnings: Do not stop at \"needs approval\"; provide the exact approval prompt Jarrad can send, with scope and boundaries already filled in. [ad-hoc note]\n\n#### 2026-06-01\n\n- BMH-OS hook boundary and local automation safety: global hooks, UserPromptSubmit, Stop, SessionStart, capture-daemon.py, explicit CLI/file handoff\n  - desc: Search this first for home-level hook boundaries, approved surviving BMH-OS integration surfaces, and when not to add cross-session automation behavior. [ad-hoc note]\n  - learnings: June 1 cleanup removed the global memory-bridge helpers/hooks; stay inside the approved hook set and prefer explicit handoff paths. [ad-hoc note]\n\n#### 2026-05-31\n\n- Claude desktop prompt timing: Claude Code desktop, paste prompt, queued turn, running turn, prepare unless confirmed\n  - desc: Use when asked to paste/send something into Claude Code desktop and the timing boundary matters. [ad-hoc note]\n  - learnings: Treat paste requests as preparation unless send timing is explicit; inspect app state first. [ad-hoc note]\n\n### Older Memory Topics\n\n#### workflow scope across $HOME/Sites/* repos\n\n- Cross-repo worktree autonomy and blocker handling: actual blocker, next safe unblocked step, other-agent worktree, Codex-owned worktree, concrete path forward\n  - desc: Covers stop-vs-continue judgment, blocker reporting, and safe progress around other-agent worktrees; cwd=workflow scope across $HOME/Sites/* repos. [ad-hoc note]\n\n- Chrome debugging and review surface: Chrome DevTools, Google Chrome, in-app browser, local dev servers, review surface\n  - desc: Covers Chrome DevTools-first debugging and Chrome-over-in-app review defaults for local/prod web work; cwd=workflow scope across local browser-facing tasks. [ad-hoc note]\n\n#### $HOME/Sites/Jitter and $HOME/Sites/Jitter-POC\n\n- Jitter PR closeout and Claude handoff: manual code review, actual diff, Claude Status Update, verification evidence, findings first\n  - desc: Use when a Jitter PR seems implementation-complete but still needs the real review/fix/verification/handoff loop; cwd=$HOME/Sites/Jitter*. [ad-hoc note]\n\n- Jitter shared nav and installed vendor tooling: shared nav, Sandra, BMH Institute, twilio-cli/6.2.4, deepctl 0.2.23, twilio-docs MCP, deepgram-docs MCP\n  - desc: Covers sibling-app nav reuse plus what Twilio/Deepgram tooling is already installed locally so the run does not ask for secrets or reinstall tools unnecessarily; cwd=$HOME/Sites/Jitter* and local host tooling. [ad-hoc note]\n========= MEMORY_SUMMARY ENDS =========\n\nWhen memory is likely relevant, start with the quick memory pass above before\ndeep repo exploration.\n"}]}}
$HOME/.codex/sessions/2026/06/03/rollout-2026-06-03T19-27-38-019e9007-505b-7900-af53-a71e27c5df82.jsonl:3:{"timestamp":"2026-06-04T00:27:42.182Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"developer","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<permissions instructions>\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `workspace-write`: The sandbox permits reading files, and editing files in `cwd` and `writable_roots`. Editing files in other directories requires approval. Network access is restricted.\n# Escalation Requests\n\nCommands are run outside the sandbox if they are approved by the user, or match an existing rule that allows it to run unrestricted. The command string is split into independent command segments at shell control operators, including but not limited to:\n\n- Pipes: |\n- Logical operators: &&, ||\n- Command separators: ;\n- Subshell boundaries: (...), $(...)\n\nEach resulting segment is evaluated independently for sandbox restrictions and approval requirements.\n\nExample:\n\ngit pull | tee output.txt\n\nThis is treated as two command segments:\n\n[\"git\", \"pull\"]\n\n[\"tee\", \"output.txt\"]\n\nCommands that use more advanced shell features like redirection (>, >>, <), substitutions ($(...), ...), environment variables (FOO=bar), or wildcard patterns (*, ?) will not be evaluated against rules, to limit the scope of what an approved rule allows.\n\n## How to request escalation\n\nIMPORTANT: To request approval to execute a command that will require escalated privileges:\n\n- Provide the `sandbox_permissions` parameter with the value `\"require_escalated\"`\n- Include a short question asking the user if they want to allow the action in `justification` parameter. e.g. \"Do you want to download and install dependencies for this project?\"\n- Optionally suggest a `prefix_rule` - this will be shown to the user with an option to persist the rule approval for future sessions.\n\nIf you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with \"require_escalated\". ALWAYS proceed to use the `justification` parameter - do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n\n## When to request escalation\n\nWhile commands are running inside the sandbox, here are some scenarios that will require escalation outside the sandbox:\n\n- You need to run a command that writes to a directory that requires it (e.g. running tests that write to /var)\n- You need to run a GUI app (e.g., open/xdg-open/osascript) to open browsers or files.\n- If you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with `require_escalated`. ALWAYS proceed to use the `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` parameters. do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n- You are about to take a potentially destructive action such as an `rm` or `git reset` that the user did not explicitly ask for.\n- Be judicious with escalating, but if completing the user's request requires it, you should do so - don't try and circumvent approvals by using other tools.\n\n## prefix_rule guidance\n\nWhen choosing a `prefix_rule`, request one that will allow you to fulfill similar requests from the user in the future without re-requesting escalation. It should be categorical and reasonably scoped to similar capabilities. You should rarely pass the entire command into `prefix_rule`.\n\n### Banned prefix_rules \nAvoid requesting overly broad prefixes that the user would be ill-advised to approve. For example, do not request [\"python3\"], [\"python\", \"-\"], or other similar prefixes that would allow arbitrary scripting.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule argument for destructive commands like rm.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule if your command uses a heredoc or herestring. \n\n### Examples\nGood examples of prefixes:\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"dev\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"check\"]\n- [\"cargo\", \"test\"]\n\n\n## Approved command prefixes\nThe following prefix rules have already been approved: - [\"cp\"]\n- [\"kill\"]\n- [\"/private/tmp/start-sandra-browser-v1-server.zsh\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"api\"]\n- [\"npx\", \"tsx\"]\n- [\"git\", \"push\"]\n- [\"git\", \"fetch\"]\n- [\"vercel\", \"env\"]\n- [\"npm\", \"install\"]\n- [\"node\", \"/private/tmp/closer-agent-relay-probe.mjs\"]\n- [\"node\", \"/private/tmp/closer-agent-reply-check.mjs\"]\n- [\"node\", \"scripts/browser-v1/local-auth-bootstrap.mjs\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"list\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"view\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"run\", \"view\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"checks\"]\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"build\"]\n- [\"open\", \"-a\", \"Google Chrome\"]\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"test:integration\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"PORT=3200 npm run dev\"]\n- [\"mkdir\", \"-p\", \"$HOME/Sites/Sandra Practice/.planning\"]\n- [\"ls\", \"-l\", \"$HOME/BMH-OS/tmp/sandra-v1-test-RESULTS.md\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"VOICE_MODE=real NEXT_PUBLIC_VOICE_MODE=real PORT=3201 npm run dev\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"PORT=3201 DOTENV_CONFIG_PATH=.env.local NODE_OPTIONS='--max-old-space-size=6144 --require=dotenv/config --require=./init-global.cjs' npx tsx server.ts\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -w -s OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN); export RAILWAY_TOKEN=$(op read \\\"<redacted-1password-ref> Secrets/Railway - Closer Lab Prod/credential\\\"); railway status\"]\n- [\"mv\", \"$HOME/Sites/Sandra\\\\ Design\\\\ System/.stitch/designs/closer-lab-pre-read-briefing/screen.html\", \"$HOME/Sites/Sandra\\\\ Design\\\\ System/.stitch/designs/closer-lab-pre-read-briefing/screen.v1.html\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"token=$(security find-generic-password -w -s OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN 2>/dev/null || true); if [ -n \\\"$token\\\" ]; then echo OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN_KEYCHAIN_PRESENT; else echo OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN_KEYCHAIN_MISSING; fi\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -w -s OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN); op item list --format json | jq -r \\\".[] | select((.title|test(\\\\\\\"Closer Lab|Browser V1|Closer|closer|browser\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\"i\\\\\\\"))) | [.vault.name, .title, .category] | @tsv\\\"\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -w -s OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN); op item list --vault \\\"BMH Secrets\\\" --format json | jq -r \\\".[] | select((.title|test(\\\\\\\"Closer|QA|Prod|Smoke|Demo|Browser|Login|Railway|Supabase\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\"i\\\\\\\"))) | [.title, .category] | @tsv\\\"\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"for b in $(git branch --format='%(refname:short)' | grep '^codex/'); do printf '%s\\\\t' \\\"$b\\\"; gh pr list --state all --head \\\"$b\\\" --json number,state,title,mergedAt --jq 'if length == 0 then \\\"no-pr\\\" else map(\\\"#\\\" + (.number|tostring) + \\\":\\\" + .state + \\\":\\\" + .title) | join(\\\"; \\\") end'; done\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -w -s OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN); op item list --vault \\\"BMH Secrets\\\" --format json | jq -r \\\".[] | select((.title|test(\\\\\\\"Closer|closer|Supabase|Railway|Production|Prod|Role Play|Browser V1\\\\\\\"))) | [.title, .category] | @tsv\\\"\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -w -s OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN); notes=$(op read \\\"<redacted-1password-ref> Secrets/Railway - Closer Lab Prod/notesPlain\\\" 2>/dev/null || true); if [ -z \\\"$notes\\\" ]; then echo NOTES_EMPTY; else echo NOTES_PRESENT; printf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" \\\"$notes\\\" | awk -F= \\\"/^[A-Z0-9_]+=/ {print \\\\$1}\\\" | sort -u; fi\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -w -s OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN); for item in \\\"Railway - Closer Lab Prod\\\" \\\"ElevenLabs - Closer Lab API\\\" \\\"Deepgram - Closer Lab API\\\"; do printf \\\"ITEM %s\\\\n\\\" \\\"$item\\\"; op item get \\\"$item\\\" --vault \\\"BMH Secrets\\\" --format json | jq -r \\\"{title, category, fields: [.fields[]? | {label, type, id}], sections: [.sections[]?.label]}\\\"; done\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -w -s OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN_RW); op item get \\\"BMH Institute - Browser V1 Fixture Accounts\\\" --vault \\\"BMH Secrets\\\" --format json >/tmp/bmh-institute-browser-v1-op-rw-item.json 2>/tmp/bmh-institute-browser-v1-op-rw-item.err; rc=$?; printf \\\"rc=%s\\\\n\\\" \\\"$rc\\\"; sed -E \\\"s/[A-Za-z0-9_+=\\\\/.:-]{24,}/[redacted]/g\\\" /tmp/bmh-institute-browser-v1-op-rw-item.err | head -20\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -w -s OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN); for item in \\\"Closer Lab - Browser V1 Owner Login\\\" \\\"Closer Lab - Browser V1 Admin Login\\\" \\\"Closer Lab - Browser V1 Member Login\\\" \\\"Closer Lab - Browser V1 Second Member Login\\\" \\\"Closer Lab - Browser V1 Boundary Login\\\"; do printf \\\"ITEM %s\\\\n\\\" \\\"$item\\\"; op item get \\\"$item\\\" --vault \\\"BMH Secrets\\\" --format json | jq -r \\\"{title, category, fields: [.fields[]? | {label, type, id}]}\\\"; done\"]\n- [\"/bin/zsh\", \"-lc\", \"export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(security find-generic-password -w -s OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN); op item get \\\"github-actions-prod-migrations\\\" --vault \\\"BMH Secrets\\\" --format json | jq -r \\\"{title, categor...\n[Some commands were truncated]\n The writable roots are `$HOME/Sites/Jitter-POC`, `/private/tmp`, `/private/var/folders/0r/4drnlg354fbbqgc9r55237j80000gp/T`.\n</permissions instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<apps_instructions>\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n</apps_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<skills_instructions>\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `$HOME/.codex/skills`\n- `r1` = `$HOME/.agents/skills`\n- `r2` = `$HOME/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r3` = `$HOME/.codex/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/telegram/0.0.6/skills`\n- `r4` = `$HOME/.codex/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/vercel/0.43.0/skills`\n- `r5` = `$HOME/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r6` = `$HOME/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/google-drive/0732ec94/skills`\n- `r7` = `$HOME/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated/slack/0732ec94/skills`\n- `r8` = `$HOME/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-crea (file: r2/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use when the user asks how to build with OpenAI products or APIs (file: r2/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required ` (file: r2/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Guide for